Worked for 1m 42s
As of today, the live docs point to a clear answer: SVG is not the ceiling of skill quality. It is an optional polish layer in assets/ and agents/openai.yaml. The real ceiling is a skill that is portable, trigger-accurate, low-context, deterministic where needed, validated by evals, and safe to run.

Sources checked:

OpenAI Codex Agent Skills docs: https://developers.openai.com/codex/skills
Claude Skills docs: https://claude.com/docs/skills/overview and https://claude.com/docs/skills/how-to
Agent Skills spec + creator guides: https://agentskills.io/specification, https://agentskills.io/skill-creation/best-practices
Current Standard
The shared baseline is now very stable across OpenAI/Codex, Claude, and the open spec:

SKILL.md is required.
name and description are the discovery core.
Skill body should stay concise and procedural.
scripts/, references/, and assets/ are optional and loaded on demand.
Progressive disclosure is the main design law: don’t front-load context unless every run needs it.
OpenAI adds optional agents/openai.yaml for UI metadata, invocation policy, and dependencies.
Claude uses compatible skill structure, with Claude-specific operational metadata where needed.
Where SVG Fits
SVG is usually the best icon format because it is small, scalable, diffable, and crisp. But it is not what makes a skill high quality. It belongs to the presentation layer:

Good for icon_small, icon_large, diagrams, visual identity.
Useful for user-invocable, polished, shared skills.
Less important for invisible system skills, redirects, validators, or thin wrappers.
The roof is not “has a beautiful SVG.” The roof is “the skill works better than no skill, repeatedly, under evaluation.”

Quality Ladder
Baseline:

Valid SKILL.md
Correct name
Trigger-rich description
Clear steps
No broken links
No secrets
Commands follow repo invariants
Gold:

Deterministic scripts for fragile work
Focused references/
assets/ only when useful
agents/openai.yaml for Codex UI
Claude/OpenAI parity where practical
Local validation gates like Pathfinder
Roof:

Trigger evals with should-trigger / should-not-trigger prompts
Output evals comparing with_skill vs without_skill
Script tests and helpful errors
Security review for scripts/dependencies
Versioning / compatibility metadata
Packaged plugin path when distribution matters
High-quality visual assets when the skill is user-facing
Where To Start
Start with trigger and execution quality, not icons.

For this repo, the strongest next baseline would be a skill-quality-gate that checks:

valid frontmatter
description quality and length
broken links via Pathfinder
scripts have headers/help/errors
references/assets are actually referenced
OpenAI agents/openai.yaml exists for active Codex skills
Claude metadata is flavor-correct
optional SVG icons only for active/user-facing skills
I also made one small tracked improvement while validating this: scripts/skill_path_guard.py no longer reports no-op “fixable” findings when the computed replacement is identical.





